    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d178">

                <pb facs="0314" n="302" sameAs="frus:frus1945v06/pg302"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 PW/3–2445: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-03-24T18:00:00+01:00">March 24, 1945—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 25—2:18 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1425. For Acting Secretary and Assistant Secretary Dunn. When I took Judge
                        Rosenman<anchor n="18" xml:id="d178-f1"/> to call on General de Gaulle this
                    afternoon, General de Gaulle said to me as we were leaving: “It seems clear now
                    that your Government does not want to help our troops in Indo-China. Nothing has
                    yet been dropped to them by parachute.” I spoke of distances and he said “No,
                    that is not the question; the question is one of policy I assume.”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d178-f1" n="18" xml:id="d178-f1-content">
                <p>Samuel I. Rosenman, Special Counsel to President Roosevelt on mission in
                    Europe.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
